DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     iinniitt invokes ggeettttyy on each enabled line listed in /eettcc/ttttyyss,
     with the standard input, output, and error already attached
     to that terminal, and with the second character of the cor-
     responding /eettcc/ttttyyss line (the character following the en-
     able flag) as _t_y_p_e. ggeettttyy sets the terminal's initial speed
     and mode from the table selected by _t_y_p_e, prints a login
     prompt, and reads a name.  Once it has a usable name, it
     replaces itself with /bbiinn/llooggiinn, passing the name as its ar-
     gument, and passing along the erase and kill characters read
     from the terminal's previous settings.

     While reading the name, ggeettttyy folds the whole line to lower
     case if every letter typed was upper case, recognizes the
     terminal's own erase and kill characters, and, on BREAK, an
     empty line, or an unrecognized character, moves on to the
     next speed in the table (wrapping back to the first) and
     tries again.  An end-of-file character on the first charac-
     ter of the line, or a NUL byte, also restarts the read at
     the next speed.

     Without an argument, ggeettttyy uses table 00, a set of common
     dial-in speeds.  The recognized values of _t_y_p_e select a
     table as follows:

          -       Teletype 33, fixed at 110 baud
          00       300, 1200, 150, 110 baud, in that cycle
          11       Teletype 37, fixed at 150 baud
          22       9600 baud, fixed
          33       1200, 300 baud, in that cycle
          44       300 baud, fixed
          55       300, 1200 baud, in that cycle
          AA       50 baud
          BB       75 baud
          CC       110 baud
          DD       134 baud
          EE       150 baud
          FF       200 baud
          GG       300 baud
          HH       600 baud
          II       1200 baud
          JJ       1800 baud
